---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const org = "https://github.com/ternmesh";
---

<Base title="Privacy" description="What the Tern app for iPhone, iPad and Mac and this site learn about you: nothing they keep.">
    <section class="wrap" style="padding-top: 4rem; max-width: 44rem">
        <h1>Privacy</h1>
        <p class="muted">Last changed 9 October 2026.</p>
        <p>
            This covers Tern Mesh, the Tern app for iPhone, iPad and Mac, and this site. The project
            collects nothing about the people who use them: there is no account, no analytics and no
            advertising. What does leave your device for the internet is a request to this site,
            when you visit it or when the app looks for firmware. Cloudflare, which serves the site,
            sees that request, as any host does. Both are set out below.
        </p>

        <h2>The app</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Your messages, contacts and groups</strong> are held by your node and kept on
                your device, so that the app can show them. They go between nodes by radio. They
                do not pass through, and are not stored on, any server of the project's.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Bluetooth</strong> is how the app talks to your node, and is used for nothing
                else.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The camera</strong>, on iPhone and iPad, reads the QR code of a contact you
                are adding. The app keeps no picture and sends none anywhere.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The internet</strong> is used for one thing: when you look for an update to
                your node's firmware, the app fetches the list of releases, and then the firmware,
                from this site. Those requests carry what any request to a website does: the
                address they come from and the file asked for, which for the firmware is the one
                for your node's board and region. They carry nothing else about you or your node.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>Removing the app removes everything it kept on the device.</p>

        <h2>This site</h2>
        <p>
            The site is static files, with no cookies and no analytics of its own. What the
            <a href="/app">web app</a> remembers, it keeps in your browser. The site is served by
            Cloudflare, which, like any host, sees the address a request comes from and what was asked
            for, and handles that under
            <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">its own privacy policy</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes and questions</h2>
        <p>
            If this changes, the new text and its date will be here, and the change will be in the
            <a href={`${org}/site`}>site's history</a>. Questions go to the project's
            <a href={`${org}/apple/issues`}>issue tracker</a>.
        </p>
    </section>
</Base>
